# Deploy a Two-Tier Hierarchy

This module moves from theory to deployment. A two-tier hierarchy is the most common PKI design for Microsoft environments: an offline Standalone Root CA at the top, and one or more online Enterprise Subordinate (Issuing) CAs below it. The root signs only the subordinate CA certificates. The subordinate does all the day-to-day issuing to users, computers and devices.

The module is split into these topics:

- **Pre-Installation Tasks (Two-Tier CA Hierarchy)** - decisions and preparation before any role is installed
- **Standalone and Offline Root CA** - what the root is, how it behaves and how to configure it
- **Demo 01: Root CA Install** - step-by-step installation and post-install configuration
- **Subordinate Issuing CA** - role, requirements and how it gets its certificate
- **Demo 02: Subordinate CA Installation** - step-by-step installation and signing
- **Certification Authority Security** - protecting the keys, roles, auditing and backups
- **Review**

---

## Why a Two-Tier Hierarchy

The root key is the most valuable asset in the PKI. If it is stolen, every certificate below it can no longer be trusted. Keeping the root offline makes it very hard to attack.

- **Containment.** If an issuing CA is compromised, the offline root can revoke that CA's certificate and sign a replacement, without replacing the trust anchor on every device.
- **Scalability and flexibility.** You can add more issuing CAs later (by region, by purpose, or by policy) without touching the root.
- **Simplicity.** Two tiers give most of the security benefit of a hierarchy without the extra cost of managing three tiers. Larger or highly regulated organisations may add a middle policy tier, but that is not needed for most.

The trade-off is that the root is only used rarely, so it must be carefully documented. Its CRL must be renewed on schedule, or the whole PKI will fail revocation checks.

