## Pre-Installation Tasks (Two-Tier CA Hierarchy)

Most PKI problems are caused by decisions made, or not made, before installation. Some settings cannot be changed afterwards, so this planning stage is the most important part of the deployment.

### Servers needed

| Server | Type | Domain joined? | Network |
|---|---|---|---|
| Root CA | Standalone Root CA | No (workgroup) | None. Offline, powered off when not in use. |
| Issuing CA | Enterprise Subordinate CA | Yes | Online, internal network. |
| Web server (CDP/AIA) | IIS hosting the CRL and CA certificate files | Usually yes | Reachable by all clients, using a DNS alias such as `pki.contoso.com`. |

The web server can be a separate machine or, in smaller labs, the issuing CA itself. For production, a separate web server is recommended so revocation data stays available even if the CA is down.

### Decisions to make first

- **CA names.** The CA common name appears in every certificate and CRL and cannot be changed after installation. Use clear names such as `Contoso Root CA` and `Contoso Issuing CA 01`. Do not put the server name in the CA name, and keep root and issuing names obviously different.
- **Server names.** Do not rename the server or change its domain membership after the CA role is installed. This breaks the CA.
- **Key algorithm and length.** RSA 4096 for the root and RSA 2048 or 4096 for the issuing CA are the common choices. Check that your applications and devices support what you choose before installing.
- **Hash algorithm.** SHA-256 or higher. SHA-1 must not be used (see Part 1).
- **Cryptographic provider.** Software Key Storage Provider (KSP) for a basic deployment, or an HSM (Hardware Security Module) for production. The provider cannot be changed without regenerating the key.
- **Validity periods.** The root's validity should be longer than the issuing CA's. A common choice is 20 years for the root and 10 years for the issuing CA. An issuing CA certificate cannot be valid longer than its parent.
- **CRL and AIA design.** Decide the URLs, CRL periods and overlap now (Part 2). The extensions must be correct before the issuing CA issues any certificate.
- **Policy and CPS.** Decide the certificate policy and Certification Practice Statement (CPS) URL, if one is required for your organisation.
- **Time.** The root and issuing CA must have accurate clocks. Time errors cause CRLs and certificates to look not yet valid or expired.
- **Accounts and groups.** Plan separate administrative accounts for CA Administrator, Certificate Manager, Auditor and Backup Operator (see the security topic).

### Environment preparation

- Build the servers with a supported Windows Server version, fully patched, and hardened according to your standard.
- Create the DNS alias (CNAME) for the PKI web site, for example `pki.contoso.com`.
- Install and configure IIS on the web server, create the virtual directory (for example `/pki` or `CertEnroll`), and prepare to enable double escaping for delta CRLs.
- Make sure the person doing the installation holds the right permissions. An Enterprise CA needs membership of the Enterprise Admins group (or equivalent delegated rights) to install and publish to Active Directory. A standalone root needs only local administrator rights.
- Plan how to move files to and from the offline root (for example a controlled USB drive or virtual disk). The root should not be connected to the network.
- Plan the backup and where the root's backup and media will be stored.

